  <iati-identifier>NL-KVK-40409352-PRJ13-123-0006</iati-identifier>
  <reporting-org ref="NL-KVK-40409352" type="21">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Netherlands Red Cross</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Multi Hazard Analysis in Conflict Settings - MHACS</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">-National Society as key partner of ICRC is able to collect, clean and disseminate data that&#x202F;is needed to improve living conditions and provide water to IDPs&#x202F; 
-ICRC is able to get answers to operational questions&#x202F;on multi-hazard risks and impacts&#x202F;to allow for timely&#x202F;&#x202F;planning&#x202F;of&#x202F;their&#x202F;operations&#x202F; 
-Set-up a data sharing agreement 
-Tested and improved measures are put in place to provide a feedback loop that feeds back into outcomes 1 and 2.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Burkina Faso, Myanmar and Lebanon</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Extreme weather and climate-related disasters have killed more than 410.000 people in the last decade. The majority lives in low- and lower-middle-income countries. This absolute number of people affected is likely to be significantly underestimated due to the lack of data, poor data collection and inequality in data monitoring between countries and natural disasters. For example, data is less available and accurate for extreme temperatures, disease outbreaks, and wildfires than floods and storms. Generating better (trend) analysis improves our ability to determine the interaction between climate change and its impact on vulnerable communities.
Supplementary to the increasing climate- and weather-related disaster, conflicts profoundly disrupt social, political and economic activities. Loss of life is not necessarily caused by the violence of the conflict but indirectly by the breakdown of systems, the spread of diseases or the lack of management of treatable conditions. Conflicts drive most often already vulnerable people into an increasingly difficult situation. Furthermore, the resilience to shocks may be already damaged or even entirely overwhelmed. The effect of conflict can have a long-term impact on the environment and people&apos;s physical and mental situations. It is not said that there is a direct correlation between climate change and conflict; it indicates that the coping mechanisms against weather- and climate-related events are less in countries with conflict. The conflict weakens the ability of the community to adapt. Another effect of both climate change and conflicts is displacement, saving people&apos;s lives. Still, it also decreases their level of safety and stability, as they leave behind their assets, homes, livelihoods and social networks.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">ICRC delegations and NS.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This project focuses on multi-hazard risk and impacts in conflict zones. Quite a number of hazards (e.g. flood, drought, epidemics) are related to weather, and their intensity and frequency impacted by climate change. This project will take the experience of 4 years of hazard impact analytics within the Red Cross Red Crescent Movement partners (National Societies, IFRC Network and the ICRC Operations). Using open-source data and technologies improves the accessibility of data for better-informed decision making as data will be produced in that collaboration, it is essential that clear and exhaustive data-sharing and data protection agreements are signed between the different parties at country level before the activities start even if it is likely that most of these data should be eventually of public interest and consequently shared beyond the parties to this collaboration.</narrative>
  </description>
